  "query_preview": "..A strong high pressure system will remain centered\nover the Arizona/New Mexico border through Friday resulting in\ndaytime temperatures in the mid 80 to lower 90s in the mountains\nto 105 to 110 at elevations below 4000 feet in our forecast area.\nA few record high temperatures will likely be set. Due to the hot\ntemperatures an Extreme Heat Warning remains in effect for the \nlower portions of the Grand Canyon and Yavapai County through \nFriday. Enough moisture will remain for the daily development of \nafternoon into the evening showers and thunderstorms, mainly at \nhigher elevations. \n\nStarting Saturday and lasting into early next week...The high \npressure center shifts eastward in response to a trough moving \nover the western states. The trough will remain just far enough \nnorth and west to allow monsoon moisture from over the Gulf of\nCalifornia to stream northward across Arizona. Look for the daily\ninfluence of daytime heating on thunderstorm development but with\nthe addition of passing shortwave disturbances interacting with \nthis moisture (classic transition season stuff). Shower and\nthunderstorms coverage will become scattered to numerous with some\nlevel of overnight activity continuing. We're confident that the \npattern will contain decent vertical wind shear with the potential\nfor organized and severe thunderstorm development. In addition, \nabout 10-20% percent of model ensemble members are pointing toward\na deeper tropical moisture tap, perhaps being pulled off a dying \ntropical system over the Pacific. If the deeper moisture tap \ndevelops then a serious flash flood threat could develop. However,\nthe timing of various players in the flash flood scenario remains\nuncertain and thus our confidence remains low. Stay tuned.\n\nLater next week...Looks like monsoon moisture lingers and\nunsettled conditions remain with at least low-grade monsoon \nactivity continuing.",
